- agent: agent-a (sub-agent W1A)
- branch: agent/a/m5-w1a-mode-best-effort
- task: TASK-077 — Best-effort RO delivery in object registry
- summary: |
    Added two optional lookup interfaces to the object package:

      - object.FederationModeLookup.ModeFor(fed) → (Mode, bool)
      - object.AttributeOrderLookup.AttributeOrder(fed, cls, attr)
        and InteractionOrder(fed, cls) → (Order, bool)

    plus the object.Order enum (OrderTimeStamp / OrderReceive). Both
    interfaces are wired into object.Options as new OPTIONAL fields
    (Federations + Orders); when either is nil the registry
    short-circuits to TSO-only delivery — the existing M0–M4 tests do
    not supply either field and remain green without modification.

    The decision logic lives in two new helpers next to the existing
    fanout code:

      - update.go::deliveryTimestampForAttributes
      - interaction.go::deliveryTimestampForInteraction

    Both follow the same contract: when the producer-supplied ts is
    non-nil AND federation mode == ModeBestEffort AND every updated
    attribute / the interaction is declared OrderReceive in the FOM,
    the timestamp is replaced with nil (RO delivery). Any deviation
    (Verbose mode, unknown order, mixed-order bundle, missing lookup)
    keeps the timestamp (TSO delivery). This matches the wire-level
    invariant in core/object.go: "ts *LogicalTime, // nil = RO;
    non-nil = TSO".

    Production wiring lives in
    rti/internal/transport/grpc/best_effort.go (NEW): FOMRepoOrderLookup
    adapts a core.FOMRepository to the object.AttributeOrderLookup
    interface by type-asserting each FOM handle for the optional
    FOMOrderResolver contract. The cut-1 fomHandle does not yet
    implement FOMOrderResolver — every lookup returns
    (OrderTimeStamp, false) and the registry stays on TSO. A future
    cut can upgrade fomHandle to read model.Attribute.Order /
    model.InteractionClass.Order without touching this adapter.

    rti/cmd/rtid/main.go now wires the federation manager and a
    FOMRepoOrderLookup into object.Options so the production server
    is best-effort-ready as soon as the FOM handle gains the resolver.

    Spec tests:
      - rti/spec/M5/best_effort_test.go: replaced both t.Skip scaffolds
        with real end-to-end scenarios (fed mgr + decl mgr + obj
        registry + recordingOutbox). Tests inspect the OutboundEvent
        via a federateEventCarrier interface assertion and check
        ReflectAttributeValues.LogicalTime nil/non-nil presence.

      - rti/spec/M5/fixtures.go: extended permissiveFOMHandle's
        adjacent helpers with an orderTable type satisfying
        object.AttributeOrderLookup. Tests call
        DeclareAttributeReceive / DeclareInteractionReceive to mark
        specific (fed, cls, attr) triples as best-effort. Existing
        permissiveFOMHandle method set is preserved; the only addition
        is the new orderTable plus an import of rti/internal/object.

- acceptance:
    - rti/spec/M5/best_effort_test.go::TestSpec_M5_BestEffort_RODelivery
      GREEN — best-effort federation + Receive-order attribute →
      ReflectAttributeValues.LogicalTime is nil on the subscriber's
      outbox event.
    - rti/spec/M5/best_effort_test.go::TestSpec_M5_BestEffort_VerboseModeStillTSO
      GREEN — Verbose-mode federation + Receive-order attribute →
      timestamp preserved (regression check).
    - go test ./... passes (M0–M4 spec + M5 spec all green).
    - go test -race ./rti/internal/... passes (no new races).
    - golangci-lint run ./rti/internal/... — no new lint issues.
    - core.FOMHandle interface UNCHANGED (M0-frozen; no contract
      change request needed because the order info is consumed via the
      new optional FOMOrderResolver assertion in the gRPC adapter).
